#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130105} :activity/id #uuid "5df21424-1e37-5c52-9fa1-bd4df936b142" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049331 :topic/id #uuid "ec6fdbaa-c722-5e5b-b092-5ba93bdf0c02" :topic/math-academy-id 2027} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130411 :tutorial/content "Suppose a massless, rigid lamina rotates with angular velocity $\\omega$ about a fixed axis $L.$ Attached to the lamina are a set of particles $P_1,P_2,\\ldots,P_n$ of masses $m_1,m_2,\\ldots,m_n$ at distances $r_1,r_2,\\ldots,r_n$ from the axis, respectively. \n\n![](images/b4/b4eeef756faf65e7caaf5bb8dad8d803cacf3e4f17b719868b647bf7e9c9da89.png)\n\nLet's find the total kinetic energy of this system.\n\nEach particle $P_i$ of mass $m_i$ has linear speed $v_i = r_i\\omega.$ So, its kinetic energy is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12m_iv_i^2 = \\dfrac12m_i(r_i\\omega)^2 = \\dfrac12m_ir_i^2\\omega^2.\n$$\n\nThe total kinetic energy of the system is obtained by adding the energies of all the particles. So, the kinetic energy of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Total KE} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{2}{m}_{i}{r}_{i}^{2}{\\omega }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{r}_{i}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the total kinetic energy of the system is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Total KE} = \\dfrac12I\\omega^2,\n$$\n\nwhere the quantity\n\n$$\nI = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n m_ir_i^2\n$$\n\nis called the **moment of inertia** of the system about the fixed axis $L.$ The units of moment of inertia are $\\rm{kg}\\cdot\\rm{m}^2.$\n\nThe moment of inertia in angular motion is analogous to mass in linear motion:\n\n- For linear motion, the kinetic energy is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{M} v^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $M$ is the (total) mass of a system of particles.\n\nThe mass of a body measures how resistant the body is to changes in linear motion. It takes a larger force to induce a fixed change in linear motion for a large mass than for a smaller mass.\n\n- For angular motion, the kinetic energy is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{I} \\omega^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the moment of inertia of a system of particles.\n\nThe moment of inertia of a body measures how resistant the body is to changes in *angular* motion. It takes a larger force to induce a fixed change in angular motion for an object with a large moment of inertia than for a body with a smaller moment of inertia." :tutorial/id #uuid "8d36ccdb-eb7d-55de-80e6-f75752dae9db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5071 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3933d4e7-a615-5065-8581-94f59b08025e" :step/math-academy-id 31761 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129211 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129030 :question/id #uuid "c317696a-899d-5af5-9bf4-2d38862f5877" :question/math-academy-id "e-8542"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92cc3d27-0e31-571f-9b92-4f209c5a08e0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426169}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina With Uniform Mass Density"} :step/id #uuid "07bfb470-2113-587d-b8ac-9dfa4cc8061f" :step/math-academy-id 31762 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129212 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129031 :question/id #uuid "bee275c6-cca6-515f-b737-597ecca278a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8543"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fdc1a2a-8a35-59f2-9890-5b835693df4f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050766}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426173}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function"} :step/id #uuid "a6003fb3-076a-5d38-ad49-10afc3a9a590" :step/math-academy-id 31765 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130106}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128862 :question/id #uuid "0a99e46a-2104-5285-862b-96b98e7a2f85" :question/math-academy-id "e-12482"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f280af-9947-5d54-9284-9ae75d46c682" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281627} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426170}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function"} :step/id #uuid "1407188f-cbba-5d61-9842-deda3b5d7b07" :step/math-academy-id 44944 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130241}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130240 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129285 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128863 :question/id #uuid "dbf6a3b7-abb9-5f62-83af-00169c673bc6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12483"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eba1f811-7b7d-56f6-8b49-4e7a4c7db81b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051016}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281644} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367867} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426165}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Moments of Inertia Using Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "83defff2-7dee-5a52-871f-690f395d1ba1" :step/math-academy-id 44945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130241 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130472 :tutorial/content "It's often convenient to calculate moments of inertia by changing from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. \n\nTo express an integral using polar coordinates, we use the change of variables formula given by\n\n![](images/fd/fdaacab5507fce09c2a1d31fe7c1aa46e80fab4c28db34574b6f214a5d4428e6.svg)\n\nApplying this formula to the moment of inertia about the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis of a plane lamina ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) with mass density function ![](images/ac/ac8c3fda52a02808979c7543290abb75797c85b3b0db8aed7a51cc2afda66995.svg) we get\n\n![](images/7d/7d3835a23ea01d7d6cab55dd71799f2b77c66f58456bdcf2b2968e9b9f09f175.svg)\n\nIf we repeat this process for moments of inertia about the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis and the origin, we obtain two more definitions. Let's summarize them below:\n\n- The moment of inertia of ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) about ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis is given by\n\n![](images/7c/7c2ed4b4705bdacaaf8c97d19a1bbf0b6ae56fe297dca5e5bdbe3c84094a1bcc.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia of ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) about ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-axis is given by\n\n![](images/17/17efc2618db89a57e67423004eba2485ca6af8cd9f71fa271f9540cd0e3c8d74.svg)\n\n- The moment of inertia of ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) about origin is given by\n\n![](images/8f/8f79b08c632cff0162fb8f4c962d44c105e34eb32e8a11cf4eca3868e0362738.svg)\n\nNote that we denote ![](images/10/10b9e6b5ce99ae6bfc544d9480ac6d62cfa9d1f23f78b8b3a331c6c517e1472d.svg) the density function expressed in polar coordinates." :tutorial/id #uuid "5d1a8a74-d440-56ad-b42b-a46e22861e20" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7766 :tutorial/title "Moments of Inertia in Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "25de40ad-47b7-51a1-b7b0-6a81e5b10517" :step/math-academy-id 44952 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130240}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130474 :tutorial/content "Let's now find a formula for the moment of inertia of a plane lamina with continuously varying mass density.\n\nConsider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y),$ (in mass/area), which depends on the position.\n\nBy choosing a finite number of points $(x_{ij},y_{ij}),$ where $i=1,2,\\ldots,m$ and $j=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we can break the region $D$ into non-overlapping boxes $B_{ij} = [x_{ij}, x_{ij} + \\Delta x] \\times [y_{ij}, y_{ij} + \\Delta y]$ of area $\\Delta x \\Delta y = \\Delta A.$\n\n![](images/c5/c5ac4166ed637b4de525f069673cc6abfaeb2e8e659b3a212d6f2f4d1bf9fc11.png)\n\nFor large $m$ and $n,$ the mass density in each box, $B_{ij}$ is approximately constant and equal to the mass density of a point $(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*)$ in the box. Hence, the mass of this part of the lamina is approximately the product of the mass density at this point and the area of the box:\n\n$$\nm_{ij} \\approx \\lambda(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*) \\cdot \\Delta A\n$$\n\nSuppose the function $r(x,y)$ gives the distance of each point $(x,y) \\in D$ from a fixed axis $L.$ Then, using the formula for the moment of inertia for a collection of masses, summing over these points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{m}_{i}[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2} \\\\  & ≈{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(\\lambda ({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})\\cdot ΔA)[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2} \\\\  & ≈{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}\\lambda ({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})[r({x}_{ij}^{∗},{y}_{ij}^{∗})]^{2}ΔA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the limit as the length and width of the boxes tend to zero, we get an exact representation of the moment of inertia as an integral:\n\n$$\nI = \\lim\\limits_{m,n\\to \\infty} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^m \\sum\\limits_{j=1}^n \\lambda(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*) [r(x_{ij}^*, y_{ij}^*)]^2 \\Delta A\n= \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about a fixed axis $L$ of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density $\\lambda(x,y)$ is\n\n$$\nI_L = \\iint\\limits_D \\lambda(x,y) [r(x,y)]^2 \\:\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(x,y)$ is the distance from the point $(x,y)$ to the axis $L.$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll limit the axis of rotation to the $x$- and $y$-axes. Note that the distance between a point $(x,y)$ and the $x$ and $y$ coordinate axes are $r_x(x,y) = |y|$ and $r_y(x,y) = |x|,$ respectively.\n\n![](images/8a/8a94fcdb8bb1a97e2bc9122e88ca7bd4f441299d2ce3c7bee819e644cf4eae89.png)\n\nHence, the moments of inertia of a plate with respect to the coordinate axes $x$ and $y,$ denoted $I_x$ and $I_y$, respectively, are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ {I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA related concept is the **moment of inertia about the origin** (or **polar moment of inertia**), which deals with the strength of an object when it is rotating. It is defined as\n\n$$\nI_0 = \\iint\\limits_D (x^2+y^2)\\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm dA = I_x + I_y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "170c6cfe-d50a-5cee-b724-5409ca4c8461" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7794 :tutorial/title "Moment of Inertia of Plane Laminas"} :step/id #uuid "3f899894-d69b-5df5-8333-4e2b804563ee" :step/math-academy-id 45104 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130107}}] :activity/title "Moments of Inertia of Laminas About the Coordinate Axes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129369}]}
